## Wiring it in (Express-style example)

```ts
const profileCache = new ProfileCache<UserProfile>({
  store: new MemoryStore(),
  ttlSeconds: 300,
  onError: (err) => logger.warn({ err }, "profile cache error"),
});

app.get("/users/:id/profile", async (req, res) => {
  const profile = await profileCache.get(req.params.id, () =>
    db.profiles.findById(req.params.id),
  );
  if (!profile) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(profile);
});

app.patch("/users/:id/profile", async (req, res) => {
  const updated = await db.profiles.update(req.params.id, req.body);
  await profileCache.invalidate(req.params.id);
  res.json(updated);
});
```

## Redis store, if you run more than one instance

```ts
import type { Redis } from "ioredis";

export class RedisStore implements CacheStore {
  constructor(private redis: Redis) {}
  get(key: string) {
    return this.redis.get(key);
  }
  async set(key: string, value: string, ttlSeconds: number) {
    await this.redis.set(key, value, "EX", ttlSeconds);
  }
  async del(key: string) {
    await this.redis.del(key);
  }
}
```

## Behaviour to check against your API

- **Viewer-dependent responses:** the key is the user ID only. If the profile response differs by who is asking (private fields, admin views), the viewer or role must go into the key, or one user will be served another's view.
- **Multiple instances:** `MemoryStore` is per process, so an update on one instance leaves the others stale for up to the TTL. Use the Redis store in that case.
- **Missing users:** a `null` result is not cached, so repeated lookups of non-existent IDs always hit the database.
- **Shape changes:** bump the `v1` in the key prefix when the profile shape changes, so old entries are not deserialized into the new type.

